this morning florida is another step  closer to banning lessons about lgbtq  plus topics in certain grades state  senators passed a controversial  education bill yesterday that forbids  instruction on sexual orientation and  gender identity in kindergarten through  third grade it has led to widespread  protests from critics who have called it  the don't say gay bill manuel bajorkas  has more from northern florida where  governor ron desantis is expected to  sign the bill into law  [Applause]  opposition to the bill grew louder in  recent days with students statewide  protesting and staging school walkouts  many carrying pro-lgbtq plus signs and  chanting the florida senate's only  openly gay member spoke out within the  chamber  i don't think y'all understand how much  courage  it takes for these children to show up  every day  but after hours of debate on the senate  floor tuesday 22 year 17 nays mr  president so the bill passes  lawmakers passed the republican-backed  parental rights and education bill which  would ban instruction on sexual  orientation or gender identity in  kindergarten to third grade parents  could also sue school districts for  violations  republican state representative joe  harding sponsored the house version of  the bill  we want focus to be on those basic  fundamental things the reading the  writing the math discussions are going  to come up we can't ban a conversation  we can't ban a discussion it's not what  we're doing critics disagree saying the  threat of lawsuits will have a chilling  effect on those discussions and the  bill's broad language could apply to any  grade  which is part of the concern parents  like todd and jeff delmay have for their  12-year-old son blake how do you see it  affecting a child like blake i can  visualize the interactions that he may  be having in class kids especially when  they're young they may kind of blurt  things out or in front of the whole  class and the teacher has to or should  respond to that to allow this  legislation to take us back and to not  only return us to a place where  we were silent on these issues  but where it's being enforced by a  penalty  it's a frightening time  nationwide there are 18 similar bills  moving through the legislatures of nine  states the u.s secretary of education  called florida's bill hateful and hinted  at possible legal wrangling if federally  funded schools are found to be in  violation of the nation's civil rights  laws nate  manny thank you
 

